# JavaScript Coding Standards

## General JavaScript Rules

-   Always add JSDoc comments in English for JavaScript functions and methods
-   Use ES Modules (`import`/`export`)
-   Follow ESLint and Prettier rules
-   Use modern JavaScript features (ES2020+)
-   For block editor files: Globals `wp` is readonly available
-   For browser files: Browser globals are available
-   For Node scripts: Node globals are available

## JSDoc Standards

Always include JSDoc comments for functions and methods:

```javascript
/**
 * Initializes the block editor functionality.
 *
 * @param {string} blockName - The name of the block to initialize.
 * @returns {void}
 */
function initBlockEditor(blockName) {
    // Implementation
}
```

## ES Modules Example

```javascript
/**
 * Handles block editor interactions.
 *
 * @param {string} blockId - The ID of the block.
 * @returns {void}
 */
export function handleBlockInteraction(blockId) {
    // Implementation
}

/**
 * Utility function for block validation.
 *
 * @param {Object} attributes - Block attributes.
 * @returns {boolean} Whether attributes are valid.
 */
export function validateBlockAttributes(attributes) {
    // Implementation
    return true;
}
```

## WordPress Block Editor Example

```javascript
/**
 * Registers a custom block type.
 *
 * @param {string} blockName - The name of the block.
 * @param {Object} settings - Block settings.
 * @returns {void}
 */
function registerCustomBlock(blockName, settings) {
    if (typeof wp === 'undefined' || !wp.blocks) {
        return;
    }

    wp.blocks.registerBlockType(blockName, settings);
}
```

## ESLint Configuration

-   Follow the ESLint configuration in `eslint.config.js`
-   Block editor files have `wp` as readonly global
-   Browser files have browser globals
-   Node scripts have Node globals
